-- title:
--   Finite map data of large unit degree on a pointed smooth proper curve
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative local Noetherian ring, let $C$ be a scheme and let $c \colon C \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ be proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, let $\varepsilon$ be a section of $c$ (an element of `SchemeHomOver (𝟙 (Spec (CommRingCat.of R))) c`, i.e. a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} R \to C$ whose composite with $c$ is the identity), and let $m_0$ be a natural number. Then there exists a `FiniteMapData` for $c$ and $\varepsilon$ whose invariant $m$ satisfies $m_0 \le m$ and for which the image of $m$ in $R$ is a unit. Such a datum consists of two opens $U, V \subseteq C$, sections $f \in \Gamma(C,U)$ and $g \in \Gamma(C,V)$ and a natural number $m$ subject to: $U$ and $V$ are affine open and cover $C$; $U$ consists exactly of the points outside the set-theoretic image of $\varepsilon$; $U \cap V$ equals the basic open of $f$ and also the basic open of $g$; the restrictions of $f$ and $g$ to $U \cap V$ have product $1$; for the $R$-algebra structures on $\Gamma(C,U)$ and $\Gamma(C,V)$ induced by $c$ via `Scheme.TwoAffineOpenCover.algebraOfHom`, the evaluation maps $R[X] \to \Gamma(C,U)$, $X \mapsto f$, and $R[X] \to \Gamma(C,V)$, $X \mapsto g$, are finite; and for every local $R$-algebra $S$ and every $s \in S$ the quotient of $S \otimes_R \Gamma(C,U)$ by the ideal generated by $1 \otimes f - s \otimes 1$ is a finite free $S$-module of rank exactly $m$.
--
--   This is the chart-wise formulation of the statement that a smooth proper geometrically integral curve over a local Noetherian ring, equipped with a section $\varepsilon$, admits a finite morphism to $\mathbb{P}^1_R$ of degree $m$, of arbitrarily large degree invertible in $R$, whose fibre over $\infty$ is concentrated along $\varepsilon$: the chart $U$ is the complement of $\varepsilon$ with coordinate $f$, the chart $V$ carries the inverse coordinate $g$, and the last clause records that the first chart is finite locally free of rank $m$ over the affine line. It is used in the construction of a relative Jacobian-type datum, via [`AlgebraicGeometry.exists_relJacobian_of_smoothOfRelativeDimension_one`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.exists_relJacobian_of_smoothOfRelativeDimension_one).
